   "stem": {
      "text": "The integral test is applied to the series \\( \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{n}{(n^{2}+1)^{2}} \\). Take f(x) to be the series' general term with n replaced by x, and the lower limit of integration to be the series' starting index. Find the exact value of the improper integral of f from that lower limit to infinity."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         1,
         4
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Take \\( f(x) = \\frac{x}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}} \\), so \\( f(n) \\) is the general term. On \\( [1, \\infty) \\), \\( f \\) is positive, continuous, and decreasing, so the integral test applies.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            "x",
            [
               "Power",
               [
                  "Add",
                  [
                     "Power",
                     "x",
                     2
                  ],
                  1
               ],
               2
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The series starts at \\( n = 1 \\), so the matching integral is \\( \\int_{1}^{\\infty} \\frac{x}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}} \\, dx = \\lim_{b \\to \\infty} \\int_{1}^{b} \\frac{x}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}} \\, dx \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "An antiderivative is \\( F(x) = - \\frac{1}{2 x^{2} + 2} \\), so \\( \\int_{1}^{b} \\frac{x}{(x^{2}+1)^{2}} \\, dx = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2 b^{2} + 2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Rational",
               1,
               4
            ],
            [
               "Negate",
               [
                  "Divide",
                  1,
                  [
                     "Add",
                     [
                        "Multiply",
                        2,
                        [
                           "Power",
                           "b",
                           2
                        ]
                     ],
                     2
                  ]
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "As \\( b \\to \\infty \\), \\( F(b) \\to 0 \\), so the integral converges to \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\). This number is the value of the integral, not the sum of the series.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            4
         ]
      }
   ],
